# 4. ARCHITECTURAL NORTH STAR

The final system should resemble:

```text
                    ┌──────────────────┐
                    │    apps/web      │
                    └────────┬─────────┘
                             │
                             ▼
                    ┌──────────────────┐
                    │  @moon/client    │
                    └────────┬─────────┘
                             │
                  REST / RPC / SSE / AUTH
                             │
                             ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      apps/server                          │
│                                                           │
│  composition only                                         │
│                                                           │
│  new MoonServer({                                         │
│    addons: [base, witness]                                │
│  })                                                       │
└──────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     @moon/server                          │
│                                                           │
│ Auth      REST       RPC        SSE                       │
│ OpenAPI   Addons     Context    Health                    │
│ Logging   Metrics    Storage facade                       │
│ Cache facade          Runtime lifecycle                   │
└──────────┬───────────┬──────────┬───────────┬─────────────┘
           │           │          │           │
           ▼           ▼          ▼           ▼
      @moon/orm    Cache API   Storage API  Observability
           │           │          │           │
           ▼           ▼          ▼        ┌──┴───────────┐
      PostgreSQL     Valkey      MinIO      │              │
                                          ▼              ▼
                                  VictoriaMetrics       Loki

```

---

# 5. THREE PRIMARY DEVELOPER CONCEPTS

Keep the domain mental model simple:

```text
MoonServer = runtime
MoonAddon  = module/application capability
Vertex     = model

```

Infrastructure concerns should be presented through stable Moon abstractions.

Domain developers should NOT need to directly understand:

```text
Fastify internals
node-postgres connection plumbing
Valkey client internals
MinIO SDK internals
VictoriaMetrics protocol details
Loki ingestion details

```

for ordinary development.

---

# 6. ONE HTTP RUNTIME

There must be exactly one canonical production HTTP runtime.

Ownership:

```text
@moon/server

```

owns transport.

Application code must not construct a second Fastify runtime.

`apps/api` must be migrated toward a thin composition root or replaced cleanly by `apps/server`.

Application code must not independently own:

```text
Fastify
CORS
Swagger
auth plugins
REST plugins
SSE plugins
ORM initialization
module installers
error handling
shutdown lifecycle
metrics endpoint
health endpoint

```

when those belong to Moon Server.

---

# 7. ONE ORM RUNTIME

Canonical persistence belongs to:

```text
@moon/orm

```

Production runtime should converge on:

```text
Vertex
Recordset
registry
context
fields
relations
ACL
record rules
transactions
schema/migrations

```

Legacy production usage of:

```text
createMoonOrm
LegacyModelRegistry
Objection compatibility path

```

must be migrated or explicitly isolated behind a temporary compatibility boundary.

Do not maintain parallel model runtimes indefinitely.

---

# 8. POSTGRESQL — CANONICAL DATABASE

PostgreSQL is the authoritative persistent relational database.

Docker service:

```text
postgres

```

must map conceptually to:

```text
@moon/orm

```

PostgreSQL owns:

```text
business records
users
roles
ACL metadata
record-rule metadata
sessions if persistent session records are canonical
addon state
external IDs
migration state
reference data
attachment metadata
audit metadata
transactional business state

```

PostgreSQL must remain the system of record for durable relational data.

Valkey must NOT become the authoritative source of business data.

MinIO must NOT become the authoritative source of file metadata.

---

# 9. POSTGRES CONFIGURATION

Prefer one canonical database configuration.

Example environment:

```text
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://moonwitness:***@postgres:5432/moonwitness

```

Inside Docker network use:

```text
postgres:5432

```

not:

```text
127.0.0.1:5432

```

unless Moon Server itself is intentionally running on the host.

Document both:

```text
Docker-to-Docker connection
Host-to-Docker development connection

```

Do not hardcode database hostname in package code.

---

# 10. POSTGRES CONNECTION OWNERSHIP

Audit duplicate pools/connections.

There should not be:

```text
apps/api DB pool
+
@moon/orm DB pool
+
another plugin DB pool

```

without explicit purpose.

Prefer:

```text
MoonServer
    ↓
@moon/orm runtime
    ↓
one managed pool/runtime

```

All transactions should derive from the canonical ORM/database runtime.

---

# 11. POSTGRES PRODUCTION SAFETY

Audit:

```text
pool size
timeouts
idle timeout
connection timeout
transaction handling
migration locks
graceful shutdown
connection leak behavior

```

Provide appropriate configuration through environment or typed config.

Do not use unsafe infinite waits.

Do not leak pools during tests or shutdown.

---

# 12. POSTGRES HEALTH

Readiness must validate meaningful database availability.

Example:

```text
/health/live

```

checks:

```text
process alive

```

while:

```text
/health/ready

```

checks at least:

```text
runtime initialized
database reachable
required migrations/schema acceptable
critical addons initialized

```

A dead PostgreSQL dependency must not produce a false healthy readiness response.

---

# 13. VALKEY — ROLE

Valkey must have a precise purpose.

Use Valkey for appropriate ephemeral/distributed state such as:

```text
cache
rate-limit counters
short-lived locks
distributed coordination
temporary OTP state if architecture requires it
password-reset throttling
realtime coordination where appropriate
session acceleration if safe
idempotency keys
short-lived derived results

```

Do NOT automatically move every session or auth record into Valkey.

Durable security state should remain durable where required.

---

# 14. CACHE ABSTRACTION

If `packages/moon-cache` already exists, inspect it first.

Prefer a Moon-owned interface approximately like:

```ts
interface MoonCache {
  get<T>(key: string): Promise<T | null>

  set<T>(
    key: string,
    value: T,
    options?: {
      ttl?: number
    },
  ): Promise<void>

  delete(key: string): Promise<void>

  has(key: string): Promise<boolean>

  increment?(
    key: string,
    options?: {
      ttl?: number
    },
  ): Promise<number>

  acquireLock?(
    key: string,
    ttl: number,
  ): Promise<MoonLock | null>
}

```

Do not require domain addons to import the Valkey library directly.

---

# 15. VALKEY ADAPTER

Target architecture:

```text
@moon/cache or Moon Server cache abstraction
        ↓
Valkey adapter
        ↓
valkey:6379

```

Configuration concept:

```text
VALKEY_URL=redis://valkey:6379

```

Even if the protocol URI uses `redis://`, code and documentation should accurately identify the service as Valkey where appropriate.

Avoid unnecessary vendor coupling in domain packages.

---

# 16. CACHE NAMESPACING

All shared-cache keys must be namespaced.

Avoid:

```text
user:1
session:1
rate:1

```

Prefer deterministic namespaces such as:

```text
moon:v1:cache:model:...
moon:v1:ratelimit:...
moon:v1:lock:...
moon:v1:session:...

```

Exact format may differ.

Requirements:

```text
collision resistant
versionable
environment-aware where necessary
application-aware where necessary

```

---

# 17. CACHE INVALIDATION

Never introduce cache without an invalidation strategy.

For model caches document:

```text
what is cached
TTL
invalidation trigger
consistency expectation
failure behavior

```

Prefer correctness over aggressive caching.

Do not cache authorization results indefinitely.

ACL/record-rule/security cache must have safe invalidation.

---

# 18. VALKEY FAILURE POLICY

Define fail-open vs fail-closed per use case.

Examples:

## Cache

Valkey unavailable may often mean:

```text
bypass cache
continue against PostgreSQL

```

if safe.

## Rate limiting

Depending on endpoint sensitivity, failure semantics may differ.

Security-sensitive endpoints should not silently become unlimited without deliberate policy.

## Distributed lock

If correctness requires a lock:

```text
Valkey unavailable
→ operation must fail

```

rather than execute unsafely.

Document these policies.

---

# 19. VALKEY HEALTH

Valkey should contribute to readiness only if it is mandatory for configured runtime behavior.

Do NOT make the entire server unavailable because optional cache is down unless configuration defines cache as required.

Expose component status through health metadata without leaking credentials.

Conceptually:

```json
{
  "status": "degraded",
  "components": {
    "database": "ok",
    "cache": "down",
    "storage": "ok"
  }
}

```

Use existing response conventions where possible.

---

# 20. RATE LIMITING

Centralize rate-limit ownership in Moon Server.

Prefer Valkey-backed rate limiting for multi-process/multi-instance deployments.

Protect at least appropriate endpoints:

```text
login
register
forgot password
OTP
reset password
expensive RPC
public write APIs

```

Do not create isolated in-memory counters if the application is intended to scale horizontally.

Development fallback may exist if explicitly documented.

---

# 21. MINIO — ROLE

MinIO is the canonical object storage implementation for self-hosted Moon Server deployment.

Use S3-compatible abstractions.

Target:

```text
Moon storage API
      ↓
S3-compatible adapter
      ↓
MinIO

```

MinIO stores:

```text
binary files
attachments
uploads
generated documents
media
large artifacts

```

PostgreSQL stores:

```text
attachment metadata
ownership
ACL
record relation
object key
bucket
mime type
size
checksum
created metadata

```

Do NOT store large file blobs in PostgreSQL unless an existing justified design requires it.

---

# 22. STORAGE ABSTRACTION

Prefer an interface conceptually similar to:

```ts
interface MoonStorage {
  put(
    key: string,
    data: Buffer | ReadableStream,
    options?: {
      contentType?: string
      metadata?: Record<string, string>
    },
  ): Promise<StoredObject>

  get(key: string): Promise<StoredObjectStream>

  delete(key: string): Promise<void>

  exists(key: string): Promise<boolean>

  stat(key: string): Promise<StoredObjectMetadata>

  signedUrl?(
    key: string,
    options?: {
      expiresIn?: number
    },
  ): Promise<string>
}

```

Use repository conventions where equivalents already exist.

---

# 23. MINIO CONFIGURATION

Prefer generic S3-style configuration rather than MinIO-hardcoded package internals.

Example:

```text
S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=moonwitness
S3_ACCESS_KEY=...
S3_SECRET_KEY=...
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

```

Docker deployment may map:

```text
MINIO_ROOT_USER
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD

```

to initialization credentials, but application credentials should ideally be explicit and scoped appropriately.

Do not assume root credentials are the long-term production application credentials.

---

# 24. BUCKET INITIALIZATION

Do not rely on a human manually opening MinIO Console and creating buckets.

Implement or document a deterministic initialization mechanism.

Possible approaches:

```text
bootstrap command
startup validation + explicit setup command
init container
deployment script
moon doctor

```

Do not silently create production resources without documenting behavior.

Prefer idempotent initialization.

---

# 25. STORAGE OBJECT KEYS

Do not trust user filenames as object keys.

Generate safe deterministic keys.

Conceptually:

```text
attachments/<model>/<record-id>/<uuid>

```

or another collision-safe format.

Preserve original filename separately as metadata.

Protect against:

```text
path traversal
key collision
filename injection
content-type spoofing

```

---

# 26. STORAGE SECURITY

Audit:

```text
upload authorization
download authorization
signed URL expiry
bucket exposure
public ACL
content type
file size limits
malware scanning extension point
object ownership
record rules

```

Files belonging to restricted records must not become publicly retrievable simply because the object exists in MinIO.

Authorization must happen through Moon's security layer unless an intentionally public object policy exists.

---

# 27. STORAGE TRANSACTIONS

Database and object storage are not a single ACID transaction.

Design failure handling.

Examples:

## Upload

```text
validate
→ upload object
→ create metadata

```

If DB insertion fails:

```text
delete orphan object

```

or register it for deterministic cleanup.

## Delete

Decide whether metadata or object deletion happens first and define compensation.

Do not silently accumulate orphaned objects.

---

# 28. STORAGE CHECKSUM

Where useful, calculate:

```text
SHA-256

```

or repository-equivalent checksum for stored content.

Store useful metadata such as:

```text
size
checksum
mime type
original filename
object key
bucket

```

This improves integrity and deterministic artifact verification.

---

# 29. STORAGE HEALTH

Readiness should validate storage if storage is configured as required.

Avoid expensive full upload/download tests on every health request.

Use an appropriate lightweight capability check.

Deep diagnostics may belong in:

```bash
moon doctor

```

---

# 30. VICTORIAMETRICS — ROLE

VictoriaMetrics is the metrics backend.

Preferred integration model:

```text
Moon Server
    ↓
/metrics
    ↓
Prometheus-compatible scrape
    ↓
VictoriaMetrics

```

or another repository-justified standard integration.

Avoid custom proprietary metrics transport if existing Prometheus-compatible tooling is sufficient.

---

# 31. METRICS OWNERSHIP

Moon Server should expose application/runtime metrics.

At minimum consider:

```text
HTTP request count
HTTP request duration
HTTP status distribution

active requests
errors

DB pool usage
DB query duration where safely observable

cache hits
cache misses
cache errors

storage operations
storage errors
storage bytes

auth success
auth failure
rate-limit events

RPC count
RPC duration
RPC errors

realtime connections
realtime disconnects

process memory
process CPU
event loop metrics

```

Do not expose high-cardinality labels.

---

# 32. METRICS LABEL SAFETY

Never use uncontrolled values as metric labels.

Forbidden examples:

```text
user ID
email
full URL with record ID
JWT
request body
object key
arbitrary model filter

```

Prefer bounded labels:

```text
method
route template
status code class
model name if bounded
RPC procedure name if registered/bounded
operation

```

Avoid cardinality explosions.

---

# 33. METRICS SECURITY

Decide whether:

```text
/metrics

```

is internal-only or authenticated.

Do not automatically expose operational metrics publicly on the internet.

Docker/network deployment should make the intended trust boundary explicit.

---

# 34. VICTORIAMETRICS CONFIGURATION

Docker currently exposes VictoriaMetrics on:

```text
127.0.0.1:8428

```

for host access.

Inside Docker Compose, monitoring components should address:

```text
victoriametrics:8428

```

Do not use host loopback between containers.

If VictoriaMetrics is expected to scrape Moon Server, create or document proper scrape configuration.

A running VictoriaMetrics container without a scrape target is NOT an integration.

---

# 35. METRICS SCRAPE DISCOVERY

Create an explicit solution.

Possible architecture:

```text
VictoriaMetrics vmagent

```

or VictoriaMetrics-native scrape configuration where appropriate.

Do NOT add another component merely because it exists in industry stacks.

Inspect repository/deployment expectations first.

The acceptance requirement is:

```text
Moon Server metrics actually reach VictoriaMetrics

```

not merely:

```text
Moon Server has /metrics

```

---

# 36. LOKI — ROLE

Loki is the centralized log backend.

Moon Server should emit structured logs.

Preferred application logging:

```text
Pino or existing structured logger
↓
JSON logs
↓
shipping layer
↓
Loki

```

Do not make domain code call Loki APIs directly.

Logging must remain backend-agnostic.

---

# 37. LOG SHIPPING

A Loki container alone does not receive application logs automatically.

Determine and implement/document a proper shipping path.

Possible choices depending on repository deployment:

```text
Grafana Alloy
Promtail if already intentionally used
OpenTelemetry Collector
Docker logging driver
application transport

```

Prefer a standard collector/shipping layer over tightly coupling `@moon/server` directly to Loki.

If no collector currently exists, explicitly identify and implement the minimal appropriate integration.

Do NOT pretend Loki integration is complete merely because a Loki container exists.

---

# 38. LOG STRUCTURE

Standardize structured fields where appropriate:

```text
timestamp
level
service
environment
requestId
method
route
statusCode
durationMs
addon
model
procedure
errorType

```

Never log:

```text
password
JWT
refresh token
OTP
reset token
authorization header
DB password
S3 secret
cookie secrets
sensitive request bodies

```

---

# 39. REQUEST CORRELATION

Every incoming request should receive a request ID.

Carry it through:

```text
HTTP logs
ORM logs where practical
RPC logs
storage logs
cache logs
error logs

```

This allows a single failure to be traced in Loki.

Prefer incoming trusted correlation ID when safe or generate one.

Validate externally supplied IDs.

---

# 40. ERROR LOGGING

Log unexpected internal errors with enough context for diagnosis.

Do not return internal stack traces to clients in production.

Client:

```text
safe deterministic error envelope

```

Loki:

```text
internal structured error context

```

without credentials/secrets.

---

# 41. OPTIONAL OPENTELEMETRY

Audit whether OpenTelemetry already exists.

If yes, consolidate around it where sensible.

Potential architecture:

```text
Moon Server
    ↓
OpenTelemetry
    ├── metrics
    ├── traces
    └── logs where appropriate

```

Do not add a new telemetry framework if existing instrumentation is simpler and adequate.

Do not replace working metrics/logging purely for fashion.

---

# 42. INFRASTRUCTURE DEPENDENCY CLASSIFICATION

Every service must be explicitly classified.

Example:

```text
PostgreSQL       REQUIRED
Valkey           OPTIONAL or REQUIRED depending config
MinIO            REQUIRED when storage feature enabled
VictoriaMetrics  OPTIONAL
Loki             OPTIONAL

```

The server must know which failures mean:

```text
fatal
degraded
ignored because feature disabled

```

Do not treat every infrastructure dependency identically.

---

# 43. CONFIGURED VS ENABLED

Feature activation must be explicit.

Examples:

```ts
cache: {
  enabled: true,
  url: ...
}

storage: {
  enabled: true,
  ...
}

observability: {
  metrics: {
    enabled: true
  }
}

```

or an equivalent clean configuration.

Do not infer critical production behavior merely because an environment variable happens to exist.

---

# 44. INFRASTRUCTURE ADAPTER CONTRACT

Infrastructure adapters should follow a clear ownership model.

Possible shape:

```text
@moon/server
├── cache facade
├── storage facade
├── metrics facade
└── logger facade

packages/
├── moon-cache
├── moon-storage       if justified
└── moon-observability if justified

```

Do NOT create packages merely to satisfy aesthetics.

If a small adapter naturally belongs in Moon Server, keep it there.

Create a package only when it provides a genuine reusable boundary.

---

# 45. NO INFRASTRUCTURE SDK LEAKAGE

Normal addons should not directly import:

```text
ioredis/redis client
MinIO SDK
AWS S3 SDK
VictoriaMetrics client
Loki client

```

for platform responsibilities.

Prefer:

```ts
ctx.cache
ctx.storage
ctx.logger
ctx.metrics

```

or another canonical Moon abstraction.

---

# 46. TARGET REQUEST CONTEXT V2

Request/application context may evolve conceptually toward:

```ts
type MoonContext = {
  uid: number | null

  requestId: string

  sessionId?: string

  locale?: string

  companyId?: number

  model(name: string): MoonModel

  cache: MoonCache

  storage: MoonStorage

  logger: MoonLogger

  metrics: MoonMetrics
}

```

Do not put unnecessary heavyweight clients into every object if architecture provides cleaner access.

The important invariant is consistent platform access.

---

# 47. AUTHENTICATION

Moon Server should provide built-in:

```text
register
login
logout
me
refresh
forgot password
reset password
verification / OTP where already supported
session revocation

```

Preserve existing correct implementation.

Do not redesign auth unnecessarily.

Security requirements:

```text
password hashing
secure random tokens
expiration
revocation
session validation
safe reset token storage
rate limiting
no secret logging

```

---

# 48. AUTH + VALKEY

Audit whether Valkey should support:

```text
rate limits
temporary OTP state
token revocation cache
session cache
login attempt counters

```

Do not move canonical durable session data from PostgreSQL without a clear benefit and migration design.

A reasonable architecture can be:

```text
PostgreSQL
= source of truth

Valkey
= acceleration / ephemeral enforcement

```

---

# 49. AUTHORIZATION

All ordinary model operations flow through:

```text
authentication
↓
uid
↓
ACL
↓
record rules
↓
ORM

```

Never:

```text
HTTP
↓
SUPERUSER_ID
↓
database

```

Superuser is reserved for explicit trusted system operations.

---

# 50. FAIL-CLOSED ROUTES

Audit route default auth.

Prefer either:

```text
auth defaults to user

```

or:

```text
auth field is required

```

Public routes must explicitly use:

```text
auth: 'none'

```

Never accidentally expose an endpoint because a developer forgot to declare auth.

---

# 51. GENERIC REST

Registered models may expose generic CRUD.

Every operation must:

```text
resolve request UID
create request context
validate model
validate fields
apply ACL
apply record rules
validate filter/domain
bound pagination
bound expansion
prevent mass assignment
execute ORM
return deterministic response

```

---

# 52. REST QUERY SAFETY

Audit:

```text
unbounded limit
unbounded offset
arbitrary order expressions
nested domain explosion
relation expansion depth
wildcard abuse
raw SQL escape hatches
N+1 query behavior

```

Set centralized sane defaults.

Avoid random hardcoded limits scattered through controllers.

---

# 53. RPC

Only explicitly registered procedures may be remotely invoked.

Preferred:

```ts
defineProcedure({
  name: 'witness.calculate_score',
  auth: 'user',
  input: ...,
  output: ...,
  handler: ...
})

```

Never expose arbitrary class/model methods over RPC.

---

# 54. ADDON CONTRACT

Target:

```ts
export default defineAddon({
  name: 'witness',

  version: '2.0.0',

  depends: [
    'base',
  ],

  models,

  routes,

  procedures,

  security,

  data,

  migrations,
})

```

Do not require normal addons to know Fastify.

---

# 55. ADDON INFRASTRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS

If an addon requires an infrastructure capability, model that explicitly where useful.

Conceptually:

```ts
defineAddon({
  name: 'media',

  requires: {
    storage: true
  }
})

```

Do not necessarily implement this exact API unless justified.

The runtime must fail clearly if an addon requires storage but storage is disabled.

Do not defer failures until the first production request.

---

# 56. ADDON DEPENDENCY RESOLUTION

Must detect:

```text
duplicate addon
missing dependency
cycle
invalid manifest
duplicate model
duplicate route
duplicate procedure

```

Dependency order must be deterministic.

---

# 57. SCHEMA + MIGRATIONS

Separate:

```text
development auto-sync
production migration
addon install
addon upgrade
schema drift detection
reference data

```

Production must not silently mutate schema because of accidental:

```text
MOON_AUTO_SYNC=true

```

or equivalent unsafe configuration.

Prefer:

```text
detect
→ report
→ require explicit migration/upgrade

```

---

# 58. MIGRATION LOCKING

For production migration/addon upgrade, prevent concurrent migration execution.

PostgreSQL advisory locks or another suitable DB-backed mechanism are preferable for database schema coordination.

Do not rely exclusively on Valkey for critical database migration correctness when PostgreSQL can enforce the lock directly.

---

# 59. REFERENCE DATA

Reference data should be:

```text
idempotent
external-ID aware
upgrade-safe
deterministic

```

Example:

```ts
{
  model: 'res.role',
  externalId: 'base.role_user',
  values: {
    name: 'User'
  }
}

```

Do not duplicate seed records on every startup.

---

# 60. REALTIME

Preserve SSE/realtime capability.

Audit:

```text
auth
subscription filtering
cleanup
memory usage
global emitter
horizontal scaling

```

If multi-instance realtime needs shared fanout, evaluate whether Valkey pub/sub is appropriate.

Do NOT implement Valkey pub/sub merely because Valkey exists.

Use it only if multi-instance realtime is a real supported deployment mode.

If realtime remains process-local, document that truthfully.

---

# 61. INSTANCE ISOLATION

Audit mutable globals:

```text
globalRoutes
global event emitter
global ORM registry
global config
singletons

```

Prefer instance state:

```text
server.routes
server.events
server.registry
server.orm
server.cache
server.storage
server.metrics

```

Tests should eventually support:

```ts
const serverA = new MoonServer(...)
const serverB = new MoonServer(...)

```

without leakage.

---

# 62. PRODUCTION CONFIG VALIDATION

At startup validate:

```text
DATABASE_URL
JWT secret quality
storage credentials when enabled
Valkey URL when required
CORS configuration
environment mode
auto-sync safety
metrics exposure
log level

```

Invalid required config should fail before accepting traffic.

---

# 63. ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT

Create/update a canonical environment example.

Conceptually:

```text
NODE_ENV=production

HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=4000

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://moonwitness:password@postgres:5432/moonwitness

JWT_SECRET=...

VALKEY_URL=redis://valkey:6379

S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=moonwitness
S3_ACCESS_KEY=...
S3_SECRET_KEY=...
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

METRICS_ENABLED=true

LOG_LEVEL=info

```

Do not duplicate variables with multiple names unless providing a documented compatibility transition.

---

# 64. DOCKER COMPOSE REVIEW

Audit the supplied Compose stack.

Expected conceptual stack:

```text
moon-server
postgres
valkey
minio
victoriametrics
loki
log/telemetry collector if required

```

The current Compose may not yet contain Moon Server itself.

If deployment intent is full-stack Docker Compose, add or document the server service appropriately.

Do not assume host-run development and Docker production are the same.

---

# 65. DOCKER NETWORKING

Inside Compose:

Use service DNS names:

```text
postgres
valkey
minio
victoriametrics
loki

```

Never:

```text
127.0.0.1

```

for container-to-container communication.

Host-bound mappings such as:

```text
127.0.0.1:5432:5432

```

are for the host.

Document the distinction.

---

# 66. DOCKER DEPENDS\_ON

Use health-based dependency ordering only where useful.

Do not assume:

```text
depends_on

```

means the dependency is fully ready unless health conditions are explicitly supported/configured.

Moon Server itself must still handle dependency connection errors gracefully.

---

# 67. DOCKER HEALTHCHECKS

Add meaningful healthchecks where practical:

```text
postgres
valkey
minio
moon-server

```

VictoriaMetrics/Loki health may also be validated if appropriate.

Do not use fake checks that merely prove a process exists.

---

# 68. IMAGE VERSION POLICY

Audit use of:

```text
latest

```

for production infrastructure.

Current examples include:

```text
minio/minio:latest
victoriametrics/victoria-metrics:latest
grafana/loki:latest

```

For reproducible production deployments, prefer pinned versions or an explicitly documented controlled-upgrade policy.

Do not blindly update versions during architecture refactor.

Record this as a deployment-hardening issue if changing versions is unsafe in the current run.

---

# 69. DATA VOLUMES

Preserve persistent volumes:

```text
postgres_data
valkey_data
minio_data
victoriametrics_data
loki_data

```

Understand backup requirements.

Not all volumes have equal importance.

Highest criticality generally:

```text
PostgreSQL
MinIO

```

Valkey may be recoverable depending on its role.

Metrics/log retention may be operational rather than business-critical.

Document accordingly.

---

# 70. BACKUP STRATEGY

At minimum document required backup scope.

PostgreSQL:

```text
logical/physical backup strategy
restore test

```

MinIO:

```text
object backup/replication strategy

```

Do not claim a production-ready backend without acknowledging durable-data backup.

Automating enterprise backup infrastructure is not mandatory for this refactor unless repository scope supports it.

---

# 71. LOKI RETENTION

Audit Loki retention/configuration.

A bare:

```text
grafana/loki

```

container with a data volume is not automatically a production logging design.

Ensure:

```text
storage configuration
retention
limits
ingestion
queryability

```

are intentional or explicitly documented as local-development defaults.

---

# 72. VICTORIAMETRICS RETENTION

Current intent includes approximately:

```text
-retentionPeriod=30d

```

Verify actual current config.

Document whether 30 days is:

```text
development default
production policy

```

Do not hardwire business policy into generic Moon Server code.

---

# 73. OBSERVABILITY DASHBOARD

Grafana is not automatically required merely because Loki and VictoriaMetrics exist.

If the repository already has Grafana, integrate it cleanly.

If not, document:

```text
VictoriaMetrics = metrics storage/query
Loki = log storage/query

```

and optionally recommend Grafana as a separate operational UI.

Do not block Moon Server backend completion on adding a dashboard product.

---

# 74. `moon doctor` V2

A useful `moon doctor` command should eventually test:

```text
Node version
package manager
database connectivity
migration state
addon graph
registry validity
duplicate routes
JWT configuration

Valkey connectivity
Valkey capability when required

MinIO/S3 connectivity
bucket existence
write permission where safe

metrics registry
metrics endpoint

logging configuration

production safety

```

Deep checks must not mutate production data unexpectedly.

For S3 write tests, use an explicit disposable diagnostic object and delete it, or perform a safe capability check.

---

# 75. STARTUP REPORT

Moon Server startup output should derive from actual runtime state.

Conceptually:

```text
Moon Server

Database        connected
Cache           connected
Storage         connected
Metrics         enabled
Logging         enabled

Addons          4
Models          37
Routes          61
RPC             17
Realtime        enabled

HTTP            http://0.0.0.0:4000
OpenAPI         /docs
Metrics         /metrics

```

Never hardcode counts.

Never print secrets.

---

# 76. DEGRADED MODE

Define explicit degraded-mode behavior.

Example:

```text
PostgreSQL down
→ server not ready

Valkey down + optional cache
→ server may run degraded

MinIO down + storage required
→ server not ready

VictoriaMetrics unavailable
→ application continues, metrics collection/export warning

Loki shipping unavailable
→ application continues with local stdout structured logs

```

This is an example policy.

Reconcile with actual architecture and requirements.

Do not create hidden inconsistent behavior.

---

# 77. TEST INFRASTRUCTURE

Backend integration tests should have deterministic dependency setup.

Possible approaches:

```text
Docker Compose test profile
testcontainers
CI service containers
existing repository test harness

```

Use the simplest existing standard in the repo.

Do not require developers to manually start five services for every unit test.

Separate:

```text
unit
integration
infrastructure integration

```

---

# 78. REQUIRED POSTGRES TESTS

Test where practical:

```text
connectivity
migration
transaction rollback
unique constraints
foreign keys
graceful shutdown
pool lifecycle
schema drift behavior

```

---

# 79. REQUIRED VALKEY TESTS

Test:

```text
set/get/delete
TTL
namespace
failure behavior
rate-limit integration if implemented
lock behavior if implemented
cache bypass when unavailable if policy allows

```

Do not write tests for features not actually implemented.

---

# 80. REQUIRED STORAGE TESTS

Test:

```text
put
get
delete
exists/stat
metadata
checksum
authorization path
invalid object key
size limits
cleanup after DB failure
signed URL expiry if implemented

```

---

# 81. REQUIRED METRICS TESTS

Test:

```text
/metrics available when enabled
disabled behavior
request counter increments
error counter
no secret values
bounded labels
no uncontrolled high-cardinality values

```

---

# 82. REQUIRED LOGGING TESTS

Test at least:

```text
structured log shape
requestId
error redaction
authorization header redaction
password redaction
JWT/token redaction

```

Avoid brittle full-line snapshot tests unless existing style uses them.

---

# 83. CI V2

Backend CI should eventually distinguish:

```text
backend-core
backend-security
backend-integration
backend-infrastructure
web-ui

```

At minimum:

```bash
pnpm --filter @moon/orm build
pnpm --filter @moon/orm test

pnpm --filter @moon/server build
pnpm --filter @moon/server test

```

If infrastructure integration tests require services, configure them explicitly.

Do not make every fast unit test depend on MinIO/Loki/VictoriaMetrics.

---

# 84. SECURITY P0

Highest-priority security work remains:

```text
remove implicit ordinary-request SUPERUSER_ID

```

Generic REST must derive actual authenticated UID.

Regression test required.

Do not defer this behind infrastructure work.

---

# 85. INFRASTRUCTURE SECURITY

Audit:

```text
database credentials
Valkey exposure
MinIO credentials
MinIO console exposure
metrics exposure
Loki exposure
host port bindings
container network
default passwords
secret logging

```

The existing use of:

```text
127.0.0.1:<port>

```

for host bindings is preferable to exposing infrastructure on all interfaces for local-only access.

Do not weaken this unnecessarily.

---

# 86. VALKEY SECURITY

If Valkey is accessible beyond an isolated trusted Docker network:

consider appropriate authentication/TLS/network policy according to deployment environment.

Do not add password configuration merely cosmetically while leaving insecure network exposure.

Security boundary must be understood end-to-end.

---

# 87. MINIO SECURITY

Avoid long-term application use of:

```text
MINIO_ROOT_USER
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD

```

when scoped application credentials can be established.

If root credentials remain for local development, document this clearly.

Never expose MinIO Console publicly by default in production without deliberate access controls.

---

# 88. OBSERVABILITY SECURITY

Operational endpoints may leak infrastructure information.

Protect or appropriately network-restrict:

```text
/metrics
debug endpoints
Loki
VictoriaMetrics
MinIO console

```

Health endpoints should disclose only information appropriate for their audience.

Do not return database DSNs, secrets, bucket credentials, or internal stack traces.

---

# 89. NO SECRET HARD-CODING

Never commit:

```text
POSTGRES_PASSWORD
JWT_SECRET
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD
S3_SECRET_KEY
API keys
private keys

```

Use environment/secret management.

If a suspected secret already exists, report location without reproducing the secret value.

---

# 90. HARD-CODE AUDIT

Programmatically search for:

```text
localhost
127.0.0.1
postgres:5432
6379
9000
8428
service names
bucket names
database names
user IDs
role IDs
MoonWitness branding inside generic packages

```

Classify each result.

Some constants are legitimate defaults.

The goal is to remove accidental coupling, not eliminate all constants.

---

# 91. FAILURE INJECTION

Where feasible, test important dependency loss:

```text
PostgreSQL unavailable
Valkey unavailable
MinIO unavailable

```

Verify the documented failure policy.

Do not claim resilient degradation without tests.

---

# 92. GRACEFUL SHUTDOWN

Shutdown order should cleanly close:

```text
HTTP listener
in-flight requests where possible
realtime subscriptions
database pool
Valkey connection
storage resources if needed
telemetry exporters
log shipping

```

Avoid hanging Node processes after tests.

---

# 93. METADATA / ADMIN BACKEND

Do not build a large admin UI during this refactor.

But Moon Server may expose metadata useful for tooling:

```text
registered models
fields
addons
routes
procedures
runtime health
migration status

```

Protect administrative metadata appropriately.

---

# 94. MOON CLIENT

Eventually expose a clean client experience:

```ts
const moon = createMoonClient({
  url,
})

await moon.auth.login(...)

await moon
  .model('wx.scripture')
  .search(...)

await moon.rpc(...)

moon
  .model('wx.scripture')
  .subscribe(...)

```

Do not redesign client SDK before backend runtime consolidation is stable.

---

# 95. COMPLEXITY REDUCTION

Actively remove:

```text
duplicate server runtimes
duplicate ORM runtimes
duplicate caches
duplicate storage clients
multiple unrelated logger systems
parallel metrics registries
pass-through wrappers
dead plugins
legacy aliases
global registries

```

Prefer one canonical owner for each capability.

---

# 96. CANONICAL OWNERSHIP MATRIX

The finished architecture should have a table equivalent to:

```text
CAPABILITY               OWNER

HTTP runtime              @moon/server
Authentication            @moon/server + @moon/base models
Authorization             @moon/orm / security layer
Generic REST              @moon/server
RPC                       @moon/server
Realtime                  @moon/server
ORM                       @moon/orm
PostgreSQL connection      canonical ORM/database runtime
Addon lifecycle            @moon/server
Base models                @moon/base
Domain models              domain addons
Cache abstraction          Moon cache layer
Valkey adapter             infrastructure adapter
Storage abstraction        Moon storage layer
MinIO adapter              S3-compatible adapter
Metrics instrumentation    @moon/server
Metrics backend            VictoriaMetrics
Structured logging         @moon/server logger
Log backend                Loki
Application composition    apps/server
Frontend client            @moon/client

```

No capability should have multiple production owners without a documented reason.

---

# 97. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES V2

## PHASE 0 — Baseline

Record:

```text
branch
SHA
git status
existing tests
existing backend build
existing Docker/infra state

```

---

## PHASE 1 — Security P0

Fix:

```text
ordinary REST using SUPERUSER_ID

```

first if still present.

Add regression tests.

---

## PHASE 2 — Backend CI foundation

Make canonical backend test/build commands executable.

Add backend gates.

---

## PHASE 3 — Runtime consolidation

Make:

```text
@moon/server

```

the only production transport runtime.

Reduce application to composition.

---

## PHASE 4 — ORM consolidation

Remove duplicate production ORM path.

---

## PHASE 5 — Addon lifecycle

Standardize:

```text
MoonAddon
defineAddon
dependency resolution

```

---

## PHASE 6 — PostgreSQL ownership cleanup

Consolidate database pools/configuration/migration behavior.

---

## PHASE 7 — Valkey integration

Implement canonical cache/runtime adapter.

Integrate only real use cases.

At minimum ensure the existing Valkey service has an intentional role or remove it from mandatory stack until used.

Do not leave phantom infrastructure.

---

## PHASE 8 — MinIO integration

Migrate storage/attachment behavior behind canonical storage abstraction.

Ensure authorization and metadata relationship with PostgreSQL.

---

## PHASE 9 — Metrics integration

Expose meaningful metrics and make them actually available to VictoriaMetrics.

---

## PHASE 10 — Logging integration

Emit structured logs and ensure a real log shipping path to Loki.

---

## PHASE 11 — Production health / doctor

Unify dependency health and diagnostics.

---

## PHASE 12 — Instance isolation

Reduce globals.

---

## PHASE 13 — CLI/DX

Implement real commands only.

---

## PHASE 14 — Documentation and deployment hardening

Update architecture, environment, Docker, production guidance, backup expectations and operational docs.

---

# 98. NO PHANTOM INFRASTRUCTURE RULE

This is a critical V2 rule.

For every Docker service ask:

```text
Who uses this?
Through which adapter?
For what exact feature?
How is it configured?
How is it tested?
What happens when it fails?

```

If the answer is unclear, classify the service as:

```text
UNINTEGRATED

```

and either:

```text
integrate it properly

```

or:

```text
remove/disable it from the required stack until there is a real consumer

```

Do NOT keep infrastructure solely because it looks production-like.

---

# 99. NO DIRECT DOMAIN-TO-INFRA RULE

Forbidden normal domain design:

```text
@moon/witness
    ↓
direct Valkey client

@moon/witness
    ↓
direct MinIO client

@moon/witness
    ↓
direct Loki HTTP push

@moon/witness
    ↓
direct VictoriaMetrics write

```

Preferred:

```text
@moon/witness
    ↓
Moon context/platform APIs
    ↓
canonical adapter

```

---

# 100. END-STATE APPLICATION CODE

The final application should remain close to:

```ts
import 'dotenv/config'

import {
  MoonServer,
} from '@moon/server'

import base from '@moon/base'
import witness from '@moon/witness'

const server = new MoonServer({
  app: {
    name: 'MoonWitness',
  },

  database: {
    url: process.env.DATABASE_URL!,
  },

  cache: {
    enabled: true,
    url: process.env.VALKEY_URL!,
  },

  storage: {
    enabled: true,

    driver: 's3',

    endpoint: process.env.S3_ENDPOINT!,
    region: process.env.S3_REGION!,
    bucket: process.env.S3_BUCKET!,

    accessKey: process.env.S3_ACCESS_KEY!,
    secretKey: process.env.S3_SECRET_KEY!,

    forcePathStyle: true,
  },

  auth: {
    jwtSecret: process.env.JWT_SECRET!,
  },

  http: {
    host: process.env.HOST ?? '0.0.0.0',
    port: Number(process.env.PORT ?? 4000),
  },

  observability: {
    metrics: {
      enabled: true,
    },

    logging: {
      enabled: true,
    },
  },

  addons: [
    base,
    witness,
  ],
})

await server.start()

```

This is conceptual.

Do not create an inferior configuration API merely to match it exactly.

---

# 101. TARGET INFRASTRUCTURE DATA FLOW

The implemented architecture must be explainable as:

```text
                        CLIENT
                          │
                          ▼
                     Moon Server
                          │
        ┌─────────────────┼────────────────────┐
        │                 │                    │
        ▼                 ▼                    ▼
       Auth             REST/RPC            Storage
        │                 │                    │
        ▼                 ▼                    ▼
     Context           Moon ORM           Storage API
        │                 │                    │
        ├──────► Valkey   ▼                    ▼
        │              PostgreSQL             MinIO
        │
        │
        ├──────────────► Metrics
        │                   │
        │                   ▼
        │            VictoriaMetrics
        │
        └──────────────► Structured Logs
                            │
                            ▼
                           Loki

```

If actual code cannot be accurately described this way or by an equally coherent alternative, the integration is incomplete.

---

# 102. TARGET DOCKER CONNECTIONS

Inside Compose network:

```text
Moon Server
    ├── postgres:5432
    ├── valkey:6379
    └── minio:9000

```

Monitoring pipeline:

```text
VictoriaMetrics
    ← Moon Server metrics scrape/export pipeline

Loki
    ← collector/log shipping
    ← Moon Server stdout structured logs

```

Do not route container traffic through host-bound `127.0.0.1` ports.

---

# 103. ACCEPTANCE — POSTGRESQL

-  one canonical DB runtime
-  ORM uses PostgreSQL through canonical ownership
-  transactions verified
-  migration strategy documented
-  readiness detects DB failure
-  graceful shutdown closes pool
-  no duplicate uncontrolled connection pool

---

# 104. ACCEPTANCE — VALKEY

-  Valkey has at least one intentional platform responsibility or is explicitly optional/unintegrated
-  canonical adapter exists if used
-  domain packages do not instantiate Valkey directly
-  TTL behavior tested
-  failure semantics documented
-  rate limiting uses shared backing if multi-instance support requires it
-  cache keys are namespaced
-  authorization correctness does not depend on stale indefinite cache

---

# 105. ACCEPTANCE — MINIO

-  storage API exists
-  S3-compatible adapter exists
-  MinIO is configured through generic storage config
-  upload/download/delete tested
-  metadata stored in PostgreSQL
-  authorization enforced
-  object key generation safe
-  bucket initialization deterministic
-  orphan cleanup strategy exists
-  secrets are not logged

---

# 106. ACCEPTANCE — VICTORIAMETRICS

-  Moon Server exposes meaningful metrics
-  metrics actually reach VictoriaMetrics
-  request count/duration/error metrics exist
-  labels are bounded
-  secrets/PII absent
-  metrics endpoint security is intentional
-  retention policy documented

---

# 107. ACCEPTANCE — LOKI

-  Moon Server emits structured logs
-  request IDs included
-  sensitive fields redacted
-  real log shipping path exists
-  logs actually reach Loki
-  Loki is not merely an unused running container
-  retention/storage behavior documented

---

# 108. ACCEPTANCE — SECURITY

-  no normal REST request uses implicit superuser
-  request UID is real authenticated UID
-  ACL works
-  record rules work
-  public auth is explicit
-  storage follows record security
-  auth endpoints rate-limited appropriately
-  secret values never logged
-  operational services not unintentionally publicly exposed

---

# 109. ACCEPTANCE — ARCHITECTURE

-  one HTTP runtime
-  one ORM runtime
-  thin application composition
-  domain addons transport-independent
-  canonical infrastructure adapters
-  no direct domain-to-MinIO
-  no direct domain-to-Valkey for platform concerns
-  no direct domain-to-Loki
-  no direct domain-to-VictoriaMetrics
-  clear package ownership

---

# 110. ACCEPTANCE — CI

Backend CI verifies at minimum:

```text
ORM
server
security
integration

```

Infrastructure integration is separately testable.

UI failures should not hide backend diagnostics.

---

# 111. ACCEPTANCE — DOCUMENTATION

Documentation clearly explains:

```text
Moon Server
Moon ORM
Moon Addon

PostgreSQL responsibility
Valkey responsibility
MinIO responsibility
VictoriaMetrics responsibility
Loki responsibility

Docker networking
environment configuration
health/readiness
production startup
backup scope
metrics
logging
storage
cache
auth/security

```

---

# 112. ACCEPTANCE — GREP AUDIT

At completion search:

```bash
rg "SUPERUSER_ID" apps packages

rg "createMoonOrm" apps packages

rg "LegacyModelRegistry" apps packages

rg "FastifyInstance" apps packages/moon-base packages/moon-witness

rg "127\.0\.0\.1|localhost" apps packages

rg "minio|S3_" apps packages

rg "valkey|redis" apps packages

rg "loki|victoria|metrics" apps packages

```

Manually classify remaining occurrences.

Do not mechanically delete legitimate framework code.

